00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:02.149 align:start position:0%
 
castle point and rochford health and  well-being strategy reset  asian whale workshop from the 9th of  september 2020  facilitated by nancy smith who is the  commissioning manager for dementia and  older people's mental health southend  castle point and rockford aging well  what do we know older people are more  likely to live with multiple and complex  long-term conditions  or with frailty or dementia the older  you are  the more activity you need the more  long-term conditions you have  the more activity you need  aging well considerations for today  frailty including falls and fractures  and long-term conditions dementia  older people's mental health and excess  winter deaths  questions to consider as you look at the  data one  what does the data tell us two what are  the key themes identified  three what additional issues or effects  has covered 19  had on aging well and how might that  differ from what we see here  life expectancy versus healthy life  expectancy  according to government data life  expectancy at birth  is 80 years for males and 83 years for  females in castle point  in rochford life expectancy at birth is  81.4 years  for males and 84.4 years for females  however health inequalities mean that  some people will spend more years of  their lives in poor health than  others the difference in life expectancy  between the most and least the private  areas of castle point  is five years for males and 4.8 years  for females  whereas in rockford the difference in  life expectancy is higher for females at  4.1 years  and 3.3 years for males between the most  and least deprived areas  this data was obtained from phe  fingertips  falls and hip fractures the 2019 jsna  profiles state that castle point and  rockford  had the second and third highest rates  of emergency hospital admissions due to  falls  in people over 65 across the essex  district  castle point had 2362.11  per 100 000 population and rochford had  eight point 2258.5 one per hundred  thousand population  the highest was basildon with two  thousand six hundred  and seventy seven point one zero the  lowest was harlow  with 1742.48  both are higher than the rate across  essex which was 2059.46  and england which was 2170.43  basel and castle point and rochford were  the only districts in essex with a  higher rate than england  the age standardized rate of emergency  hospital admissions due to hit fractures  among over 65  was lower than the rate across essex as  a whole which was 611.9  however both castle point and rockford  had rates that were higher  than england which was 577.76  with 588.79 and 607.61  this is the 2017-18 data from the  hospital episode statistics  the figures stated are age standardized  rates of emergency hospital admissions  due to falls and hip fractures values  given are per 100 000 population  physical activity in older adults 75  plus  the latest comparable data from sports  england's active  lives survey shows that 56.4 percent of  adults in castle point  and 49.5 in rochford are inactive  this means doing less than 30 minutes of  physical activity a day  we have been monitoring the impact of  kovid 19 on physical  cognitive and emotional well-being  across southeast essex  from the start of lockdown and have seen  considerable deterioration across older  people  we have also seen considerable  deterioration in residents of care homes  causing upset to visiting families  numbers of people referred for the full  prevention program in castle point and  rockford in the last year  are not available but the future is  hopeful  physical activity opportunities since  march at the start of lockdown many  organizations shifted their mode of  delivery to online  offering a range of virtual activity  sessions active essex launched a youtube  channel called keep ethics active  with a weekly timetable of activities  for people of all ages  sport for confidence occupational  therapists and sport coaches delivered  sessions on zoom to offer specialist  support at home  some group face-to-face sessions have  since resumed where possible  such as the action for family carers  feeling good caring well  well-being walks for carers dementia  figures  compared to pre-covered figures recorded  in february the latest figures from july  show that the number of people recorded  with dementia  in castle point and rockford has  decreased from 1836  to 1780. meanwhile the estimated number  of people with dementia has increased  from 2800 2828 to 2849  the number of people being supported has  also increased from 972 to 1266.  the percentage recorded in february was  64.9 percent  and in july was 62.5 both of which are  below the nhs england target  of 66.7 percent for southend a total of  1764 people were recorded in july  and 238 estimated  compared to 1909 recorded  and 2405 estimated to have dementia in  february  across the whole of the mid and south  essex area the percentage recorded has  dropped from 65.8 percent in february  to 61.8 percent in july  this is the same as a national picture  with 63.2 percent of over 65s fought to  have dementia in july  compared to 67.6 in february  nancy smith commented on this saying we  have put a lot of effort into continue  to diagnose  people remotely in ways that are safe  and acceptable to people  but completely different to what they  are used to  projections according to the projecting  older people's population information  the number of people in castle point and  rockford with dementia age 65 and over  is projected to increase by 46 by the  year 2035.  although a number of people under 65 of  dementia is projected to stay the same  at around 50 people in castle point and  rockford  combined how much can we rely on these  figures  it's probably somewhat debatable it also  doesn't take into account people with  mild cognitive  impairment who might be on the register  as well  louisa morris commented on this data  saying we need to be mindful of  statistics as they are going to be a lot  bigger than those shown here  spencer dinesh commented on the dementia  data saying  when talking about dementia we only talk  about the over 65s but it is important  to support all ages  older people's mental health 20 of those  living in a community and 40  of those living in a care home have  depression numbers using  improved access to psychology therapies  is 138 since april  with the biggest increase in july and  august some of the particular problems  for older people experiencing depression  are as follows  physical symptoms and depression some  physical illnesses can give you symptoms  that are similar to those in depression  for example loss of appetite or poor  sleep can be caused by thyroid problems  heart disease or arthritis long-term  illness  if you become depressed you may start to  get more upset by your health  even though it hasn't really changed for  the worse treating the depression  can't take away any physical health  problems but it can make them much  more bearable confusion and memory  problems  depression worry and anxiety can affect  your memory  and make you feel confused you may worry  that you are  suffering from dementia when it is  actually just depression  a new sense of loneliness living alone  does not automatically make you  depressed  but feeling more lonely for no obvious  reason may be a sign of depression  depression in older people can be caused  by physical health problems and  long-term conditions  low mood and memory problems feeling  less interested in everyday activities  declining social invitations and  bereavement for carers  isolation and loneliness exacerbates the  feelings of depression and anxiety  excess winter deaths excess winter  deaths are defined as the excess winter  deaths measured as the ratio of extra  deaths from all causes  that occur in the winter months compared  with expected number of deaths  based on the average of the number of  non-winter deaths  expressed as a percentage government  data from 2017 to 18  shows that excess winter deaths are  higher in castle point at 43  and ruptured at 45.3 than the england  and east of england values  the excess winter deaths index for  people aged 85 plus  is higher in ruptured at 76 compared to  43.4 in castle point  both areas are higher than the england  and east of england values  according to the office of national  statistics colder than average  temperatures for the winter months of  2017 to 2018  may partially explain the increase in  winter deaths however previous years  demonstrate a weak relationship between  temperature and  excess winter deaths increased excess  winter deaths are not always coupled  with unusually cold winters and  conversely winters  with decreased excess winter deaths are  not always coupled with  milder winter temperatures indicating  factors other than temperature  such as influenza are also relevant in  explaining trends  in excess winter mortality  winter worries the following are worries  that have come to our attention when  thinking about the effect of winter  flu season longer darker evenings  cognitive decline falls emotional  decline  isolation loneliness depression  anxiety second wave of covid  lockdown and associated problems lack or  loss of community services and  opportunities  mental health and family and friends  other factors to consider are  wider determinants of health deprivation  housing conditions  fuel poverty barriers that may present  challenges but also opportunities  and direct and indirect effects of  covid19  questions and considerations what does  the data tell us  what are the key themes identified what  additional issues or effects has covered  19 had on aging well  and how might that differ from what we  see here  how can we improve access to services  for older people  sue mari commented on that saying kovid  has changed what people thought was  going to be their lives  she also commented in this discussion  saying we have seen a huge reduction in  the number of people coming to us for  debt advice  nancy smith suggested that we need to  acknowledge the number of older people  with debt problems  while louise and moss believes we need  to personalize things even more  it's not as simple as asking for one  thing  next steps what other data do we need to  have to inform and provide more insight  who else needs to be involved we will be  meeting as a subgroup before the  december board meeting to continue  discussion  and develop the narrative around this  priority and start thinking about some  actions  how are we tackling the problems and  what other actions would help us to  address them  in terms of early intervention and  prevention  counselor julie gooding commented that  the hub has proven that we can all work  together  sue murray added that partnership work  has become essential  and suggested we need to give a holistic  approach

